WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:14.093
DG32-2DOM is the attention variant of DG32-LITE, and its preliminary datasheet reads like DG32-LITE's with one addition: an INT8 attention engine on its own clock.

2
00:00:14.643 --> 00:00:19.526
This deck covers what the datasheet adds, and confirms what it leaves unchanged.

3
00:00:20.076 --> 00:00:26.451
Every figure is a design value, a process nominal or an analytic estimate, and each is labelled.

4
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.270
Here is the short version.

5
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.096
The same sixty-four pins and forty-four signals.

6
00:00:33.646 --> 00:00:37.292
The same one point eight and three point three volt rails.

7
00:00:37.842 --> 00:00:45.011
One added clock: the engine runs at one hundred fourteen megahertz, beside the fifty megahertz control domain.

8
00:00:45.561 --> 00:00:50.560
For a board, DG32-2DOM is a drop-in DG32-LITE.

9
00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.149
For firmware, it adds one memory-mapped engine.

10
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.950
The datasheet lists five new features.

11
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:05.283
An attention accelerator that computes a band of query rows in one kick, bit-exact to the golden model.

12
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:09.858
A shared datapath with a weight table and a forty-bit numerator.

13
00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:14.648
A cost of about thirty-two hundred cycles per row, by analysis.

14
00:01:15.198 --> 00:01:16.721
A second clock domain.

15
00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:19.416
And a larger die of its own.

16
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.859
Everything else is unchanged.

17
00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:34.361
The package, the pin groups, the rails and their limits, and the boot flow are identical to DG32-LITE, because the engine is internal and adds no pads.

18
00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:44.635
The datasheet repeats the same zero point four three watt estimate at fifty megahertz, and it does not yet state a separate figure for the engine.

19
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.104
The engine runs on a second clock, at one hundred fourteen megahertz.

20
00:01:50.654 --> 00:01:56.928
The CPU, the bus and the peripherals stay at fifty, so the control core's timing is untouched.

21
00:01:57.478 --> 00:02:03.262
The engine reaches memory through two-flop clock bridges, so it never stalls the control core.

22
00:02:03.812 --> 00:02:11.188
Timing closes with positive slack at both clocks, on a die of three point four by four point five millimetres.

23
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.154
Firmware drives the engine in four steps.

24
00:02:15.704 --> 00:02:19.454
Tell it where the query, key, value and output buffers live.

25
00:02:20.004 --> 00:02:24.159
Set the geometry: rows, keys, and the key and value sizes.

26
00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:28.201
Set start, optionally reusing the buffers already loaded.

27
00:02:28.751 --> 00:02:31.951
Then poll for done, or take the engine's interrupt.

28
00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:37.642
The engine also reports a rejected geometry or an arithmetic overflow.

29
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.192
Its limits are fixed in the silicon.

30
00:02:41.742 --> 00:02:48.360
Up to sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-five query rows per kick, and up to four hundred keys per head.

31
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.735
Key vectors up to thirty-two bytes, and value vectors up to sixty-four.

32
00:02:54.285 --> 00:03:04.359
Sixteen multiply lanes, a two-hundred-fifty-six entry weight table, and a forty-bit numerator that stays exact to one hundred thirty-one thousand keys.

33
00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.653
For the board, nothing changes.

34
00:03:08.203 --> 00:03:16.263
The footprint, the pin map, the supplies and their sequencing, and the boot flash and console are the same as DG32-LITE.

35
00:03:16.813 --> 00:03:19.650
The engine adds no board signals at all.

36
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.089
The chip's status is design complete, and in physical trials.

37
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.142
Three things to take away.

38
00:03:27.692 --> 00:03:30.519
The board is a DG32-LITE board.

39
00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:36.350
The engine is one memory-mapped block on its own clock that cannot stall control.

40
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:44.608
And its limits are already fixed: four hundred keys, sixty-four-byte values, and about thirty-two hundred cycles per row.

41
00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:48.989
First silicon turns these design values into measurements.

